winewayland: take each output's work area from KWin (every panel on its own edge) (Neutron#61)

Neutron#61, the reporter: "trying to interact with anything in the LRC window, including the title-bar, moves the
window". Lightroom Classic restored its saved place below the screen; Win32 and KWin then disagreed about where the
window was, every click landed on the bottom row of Wine's rect = the caption, and the caption started a compositor move.
227a72 (placement-bounds) and 227a71 (placement-workspace) follow Windows' rules; this patch gives those rules the
work area Windows would have.

Windows reference. rcWork = the monitor minus the taskbar on the edge the taskbar actually is on. Round 54 (winpl.exe)
and round 55 (dlg.exe) on the owner's PC, 3840x2160 at 225 %, taskbar at the bottom: work area 0,0-3840,2070 (bottom
90 px excluded, top 0); WINDOWPLACEMENT's workspace offset is then 0.

Mechanism (before). Wayland has no work-area protocol: display.c measured only how much the panels take (a maximized
configure's height) and always took it off the TOP. With KDE's default BOTTOM panel (38 logical px at 150 %) rcWork was
(0,57)-(3840,2160) where Windows has (0,0)-(3840,2103): the reporter's saved rect was moved only thanks to the wrong +57
offset, and Lightroom's own saved place (30,86)-(3810,2130) came back 57 px lower, 27 px past the bottom of the screen.

Fix (KDE Plasma only; new dlls/winewayland.drv/wayland_workarea.c, hooks in display.c, dllmain.c, unixlib.h,
waylanddrv_main.c, waylanddrv.h, Makefile.in). The exact answer is KWin's workspace.clientArea(MaximizeArea, output,
desktop). When XDG_CURRENT_DESKTOP names KDE, the desktop process (explorer.exe /desktop) loads a KWin script once
(org.kde.KWin /Scripting, the interface KWin's own scripting tools use); the script reports every output's geometry and
maximize area with callDBus back to that desktop process's own bus name, and again when outputs, the current desktop or
a panel is added, removed, moved, resized, shown or hidden, read after KWin's rearrange and only when the result changed
(a panel = a dock, or a window outside the normal layer that cannot be moved: a layer-shell surface). Only calls from the
owner of org.kde.KWin are taken. The first answer is waited for in DllMain (bounded 500 ms; measured 1-3 ms), before the
first monitor list is built; a later change rebuilds the monitor list as an output change does. rc_work = rc_monitor
minus KWin's four insets (logical -> device px); a maximized height measured under the CURRENT area and smaller than it
(a compositor-drawn title bar) is still reserved at the top, as before, so a maximized window keeps matching its
configure; a height measured before the area last changed is ignored. Each script is named after its process and
unloads itself once that process's bus name is gone (checked at every ordinary window add/remove and every panel/output
event): two desktop processes can start for one session (explorer loads the driver before it creates the desktop
window; the loser exits), so no process ever touches another one's script. KWin's script id is the count of loaded
scripts; when that id's object path is still taken (a script unloaded in the middle), the script is loaded again (up to
8 tries) and the copy that owns its path runs; the others never ran and are unloaded. libdbus is dlopen'ed (as
wayland_sni.c does): 231.1 has none of the 229 line's KWin/libdbus code.

Kill switch: NEUTRON_WORKAREA_EDGES=0 (no script, no D-Bus: the measured top inset as before).

What stays imperfect / not covered:
- Non-KDE compositors (GNOME/Mutter, Hyprland, Sway, ...), no session bus or libdbus, KWin refusing the script, or an
  output KWin does not name: the measured TOP inset as before. Right for GNOME's top bar; a bottom bar on Hyprland/Sway
  still comes out at the top (wrong), and a side bar is not seen at all.
- A panel change that keeps every panel window's geometry and visibility (e.g. a switch to "windows can cover") is seen
  at the next reported event; the script of a desktop process killed without exit stays loaded in KWin until the next
  window opens or closes on that desktop; only the current virtual desktop's area is reported; no
  WM_SETTINGCHANGE(SPI_SETWORKAREA) is sent on a change (Wine never sent one; maximized windows follow KWin's own
  reconfigure).

Line: public pre-release 11.10-231.1 (release-231.1 = Latest 231 + a70 tpidle + a71 wplws + a72 plbound + this a73);
applies after a72 on its source as built (b3, -F0, no offset/fuzz). Main has NOT got this patch (main only gets what is
folded for 230; lab copy for the 229 line: lr61i workarea-edges-229.mypatch).

Lab evidence (lr61i, 11-26; nested KWin 6.8.3, his kwinrc copied, desk 3840x2160 @150 % unless noted; lab runtime
11.10-231.1b = the earlier 231.1 dist + these winewayland.so/.drv x2; n per arm):
- winpl (round-54 probe) bottom 38 px panel: rcWork (0,0)-(3840,2103), 0/32 lines off Windows' rule, n=2; top 38:
  (0,57)-(3840,2160) 0/32; left 48: (72,0)-(3840,2160) 0/32 (an earlier build took the old panel's maximized height for
  a title bar there: fixed by the "measured under the current area" rule). Kill switch: (0,57)-(3840,2160) = before.
- Lightroom Classic 16.0.1, bottom panel: the reporter's saved rect (2480,2137)-(4400,3189) now lies entirely below the
  work area and LrC itself opens at its own default (30,30) 3780x2043 (inside (0,0)-(3840,2103)); photo clicks reach the
  loupe, 0 compositor moves, n=2 + maximized->restore n=1. LrC's own place (30,86)-(3810,2130) stays (30,86) (was shifted
  to (30,143)), n=2.
- Panel moved while an app runs (bottom -> top -> left -> bottom): rcWork followed within ~1 s each time, n=1.
- His layout (3840x2160 @175 % + 1920x1080 @100 %, top 32 px panel, no-strut bottom dock): DISPLAY1 (0,56)-(3840,2160),
  a panel-less DISPLAY2 = its whole monitor (kill switch: the 32 px estimate), n=1. Photoshop / Illustrator maximized =
  KWin's area exactly (0,32 2194x1202 logical), Open/Save As identical to 231, n=1.
- Start-up: the script answers in 1-3 ms (>30 starts); cold `wine` start 231.1 vs 231.1b alternating n=4: median 1335 vs
  1322 ms.
Untested: his real Plasma session (real plasmashell panels are docks: covered by the filter), a real second-monitor
panel (the lab's panels all land on output 0), GNOME/Hyprland/Sway runs (the code path is off there).

